# Line-ending and diff policy, pinned per repository.
#
# Why this file exists: without it, line endings are decided by each machine's
# `core.autocrlf`, which is a global setting that differs between the two
# clients we run. Windows git had `core.autocrlf=true` (system-wide) while WSL
# git had it unset, so the SAME bytes were "clean" on one side and "every file
# modified" on the other. A fresh checkout made by Windows git wrote CRLF, and
# WSL git then read those CRLF bytes against an LF index and reported all 57
# files changed. Pinning the policy here means the answer no longer depends on
# whose machine you are sitting at.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text and normalise it to LF *in the repository*.
# `eol=lf` makes the WORKING TREE LF on every platform too, so a file is
# byte-identical on Windows and Linux and cross-platform hash comparisons are
# meaningful. Python, YAML, TOML and Markdown are all LF-safe on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit binary markers: never normalise, never diff as text. Nothing in the
# current tree is binary, so these are here to keep a future asset from being
# silently rewritten by the rule above.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.tar   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.so    binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.onnx  binary

# Windows-only scripts, if any are ever added, keep CRLF so their own tooling
# is happy; everything else in the repo is LF.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
